d that  so that you could imagine an economic  and monetary union would include not  only a monetary union but a banking  union of fiscal union and economic union  and they're trying to create all of that  but they're doing it on the fly you  might say in an ad hoc post hoc way  responding to the crisis and in some  sense it reflects the shortcomings  in the initial design you might say  design flaws of EMU as it now exists and  they recognize that and they're moving  toward it and in fact we'll be talking  about that in the next european council  meetings but when will those be well  there'll be a meeting actually there's  one next week and then there's one in  december the european council of the  heads of state and government of the  member states and they are looking at  the possibility of a treaty at some  point in the future that would move them  some distance closer to that goal but  it's still a goal in the future how far  in the future do you think a long way in  the factor and a long way in the future  in part because a treaty requires  ratification by all of the Member States  and while and that means a public  support in some cases there are some  countries that would many countries  actually that would reveal they had to  have a referendum on a treaty that was  actually creating say a quote genuine  EMU which is what is now being discussed  in the European Council and we've had  previous treaties that have been  defeated in referendums the public has  lost a great deal of trust in the EU as  a result of this crisis and there are  further complications as well for  example the German Constitutional Court  would have to approve any treaty and it  has been of all the actors in this  crisis the one that has most  consistently argued for protecting  democratic rule but in the states as  they move toward greater supranational  control over economic fiscal monetary  policy  and how do you create a fiscal Union a  banking Union and economic union moving  those 